.TH "gpipe" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
gpipe \- build and inspect the router's packet-processing pipeline

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
gpipe list
gpipe add module [argument]
gpipe clear
gpipe trace destination_ip

gpipe cp add lua /scripts/file.lua [tick_ms]
gpipe cp list | status | stop

gpipe mcast join | leave group interface_number
gpipe mcast show
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

Every IP packet the router FORWARDS goes through the same stages: it is parsed, it passes the
pipeline modules in order, the route table picks an output interface, and the frame is rewritten
for the next hop. The modules are the part you control. Each one looks at the packet and either
lets it CONTINUE to the next stage or DROPs it, so the order matters: the first module to drop a
packet is the one that stopped it.

The pipeline is empty at start, which is plain forwarding. Modules see only forwarded packets,
after the TTL has been checked: packets addressed to the router itself (a ping to one of its
interfaces), ARP, and multicast do not pass through them. In OpenFlow mode the flow table decides
instead and the pipeline is not used.

.B list
prints the pipeline as the router runs it. The number in each box is the module's position:
.RS
.nf
base: parse -> [0:acl:10.0.3.0/24] -> [1:block:10.0.2.10] -> route -> rewrite
.fi
.RE

.B add
appends a module to the END of the pipeline.
.B clear
removes every module. There is no command to remove one module: clear, then add back the ones
you want, in the order you want. The Router Lab's module editor does exactly that for you.

.B trace
asks "what would the pipeline do with a packet to this address?" without sending one. It runs a
made-up packet whose only field is the destination address through each module and prints each
verdict, stopping at the first DROP. Because the rest of the packet is zeros, a module that looks
at other fields (a Lua script reading pkt.proto, say) may answer differently for real traffic,
and counter and rate count the trace packet as one of theirs.

.SH MODULES

.B acl
cidr
.RS
Drop every packet whose destination is inside cidr (a deny rule).
.nf
gpipe add acl 10.0.3.0/24
.fi
.RE

.B block
ip
.RS
Drop packets to exactly one destination address: the same job as acl with a /32, written as a
small self-contained C module (gr_mod_block.c in the GINI source). It is the example to copy
when you write a native module of your own.
.RE

.B nat
ip
.RS
Rewrite the SOURCE address of every packet to ip and fix the IP header checksum. The TCP/UDP
checksum is not adjusted, and it covers the source address, so the receiver discards rewritten
TCP (and UDP that carries a checksum) as corrupt; ping (ICMP) works.
.RE

.B rate
pps[/burst]
.RS
A token-bucket policer: let through at most pps packets per second, allowing a burst of up to
burst packets (default: one second's worth), and drop the rest. It drops rather than delays; to
add latency, use the delay command.
.nf
gpipe add rate 100/200
.fi
.RE

.B classify
[cidr:]dscp
.RS
Mark packets to cidr (or all packets) with a DSCP value in the IP header: a name (be, ef,
cs0..cs7, af11..af43) or a number 0..63. It only marks; nothing in the pipeline treats marked
packets differently.
.nf
gpipe add classify 10.0.3.0/24:ef
.fi
.RE

.B tap
path[@cidr]
.RS
Copy each packet (or only those to cidr) into a pcap file that opens in Wireshark, and let the
original continue untouched. Use a path under /captures, which is ~/.gini/captures on your
computer. If path is an existing named pipe (mkfifo), packets stream live to whatever reads it.
.nf
gpipe add tap /captures/r1.pcap@10.0.2.0/24
.fi
.RE

.B counter
.RS
Count the packets that reach this point and let them all continue.
.RE

.B filter
.RS
Apply the router's classic firewall, the rules made with the filter command, at this point.
.RE

.B lua
/scripts/file.lua
.RS
Run your own Lua function on every packet. Scripts live in ~/.gini/scripts on your computer,
which the router sees as /scripts. The script defines process:
.nf
function process(pkt, ctx)
  if pkt.dst == "10.0.3.10" then return DROP end
  return CONTINUE
end
.fi
pkt has the fields src and dst (dotted strings), proto, ttl and len. A script can call
log(text); its output goes to the router's log (View logs in gBuilder), not to this console.
.RE

.SH CONTROL PLANE MODULES

A pipeline module can only judge a packet that is passing through. A protocol has to act on a
clock, send its own packets and write the route table, which is what a control-plane module is
for. It runs alongside forwarding rather than inside it.

.B cp add lua
/scripts/file.lua [tick_ms]
.RS
Load a Lua control-plane script. It may define init(ifaces), tick() (called every tick_ms) and
on_message(iface, src, payload, pkt), and it can call listen, send, emit, route_add, route_del,
interfaces, log and publish. rip_reference.lua and mcast_tree.lua, which gBuilder puts in
~/.gini/scripts, are complete worked examples.
.RE

.B cp list
names the loaded modules,
.B cp status
prints what each has published, and
.B cp stop
removes them all.

.B mcast join
and
.B leave
add or remove interface_number (1 for tun1) as a member of a multicast group;
.B mcast show
prints the membership table.

.SH EXAMPLES

Drop everything to one subnet, count what is left, and check two destinations:
.RS
.nf
gpipe add acl 10.0.3.0/24
gpipe add counter
gpipe list
gpipe trace 10.0.3.7
gpipe trace 10.0.2.10
.fi
.RE

Start again, with a capture of the traffic towards 10.0.2.0/24:
.RS
.nf
gpipe clear
gpipe add tap /captures/r1.pcap@10.0.2.0/24
.fi
.RE

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR watch (1G),
.BR delay (1G),
.BR filter (1G),
.BR route (1G)
